## Process- och operativsystemprogrammering
Practiced concurrency programming with a labpartner from University. 

The project involved extending and working with Pintos, an educational operating system written in C,
to explore core operating-system concepts.

## What We Worked On

- C programming and memory management

- Pintos operating-system development

- System calls

- User programs and kernel interaction

- Process creation and management

- exec, wait and exit

- File operations and file systems

- Threads and concurrent execution

- Locks and critical sections

- Semaphores and synchronization

- Race conditions

- Deadlocks

- Safe handling and validation of user memory

- Debugging with GDB

- Testing and troubleshooting

- Project Structure 

## Pintos:

pintos/
├── threads/    # Threads and synchronization
├── userprog/  # Processes and system calls
├── filesys/   # File-system functionality
├── examples/  # Example user programs
└── tests/     # Tests for implemented functionality
Main Concepts
System Calls

Implemented and worked with the interface between user programs and the operating-system kernel.
This included handling system-call arguments, return values, files and processes.

## Process Management

Worked with creating and managing processes, including parent/child relationships and process lifecycle operations such as:

exec → start process
wait → wait for child
exit → terminate process
Synchronization

## Implemented synchronization mechanisms to safely handle concurrent execution and shared resources.

- Locks

- Semaphores

- Critical sections

- Condition variables

- Race conditions

- Deadlocks

Studied how deadlocks can occur when multiple threads wait for resources held by each other, 
and how synchronization design can prevent these situations.
